    <origin>Register ISS-019 (#998) + ISS-020 (#999), plus ISS-026 (#1006)'s READ half. Owner decision D-2 (2026-09-18/19): part 1 YES — bound the additive path; part 2 NO — the ethical wall keeps binding a former member; part 3 count before deploy; part 4 the writer is task 117. Consolidated because 109 and 110 are ONE change: a SECOND query for the veto path would re-introduce the two-snapshot hazard task 043 deliberately removed.</origin>
  </metadata>

  <prompt>One junction read feeds two consumers whose safe failure directions are opposite. Today a query-level fault is swallowed into an empty list, which the additive term reads correctly as "grant nothing" and the FR-23 deny-veto reads WRONGLY as "belongs to no organization" — so the wall silently stops matching. The same read also ignores `sprk_enddate`, so a membership ended by date still confers access. Fix both in one read that projects what both consumers need and reports when it could not be completed.</prompt>

  <role>SPAARKE platform developer — the external-access evaluator's membership path. You are changing who has access today AND a veto's fail direction; treat the narrowing as an access-removal event and the veto as fail-closed-or-nothing.</role>

  <goal>One junction read returns an outcome carrying `Unreadable` plus TWO named sets — a date-bounded conferring set and a statecode-only wall-subject set — so the additive term cannot over-grant, the veto cannot stop matching on a fault, a date-ended membership stops conferring, and an inactive organization confers nothing. Task 043's single-snapshot invariant and the NFR-02 one-read budget both survive.</goal>

  <context>
    <background>🟢 THE DILEMMA DISSOLVES — AND MOST OF THE MACHINERY ALREADY EXISTS. The recorded framing was "one query serves an additive term (over-inclusion = over-GRANT) and the deny-veto subject (over-inclusion = a stricter wall), so one filter shape cannot be right for both." That is true only because the query projects BARE IDS. It is one READ, not one FILTER: add `sprk_enddate` to the `$select` and one read serves both consumers with different IN-MEMORY predicates.

⚠️ DO NOT INVENT AN OUTCOME TYPE. `ActiveOrgMemberships(IReadOnlyList&lt;Guid&gt; OrganizationIds, bool Unreadable)` ALREADY EXISTS at `AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:457`, and its remarks (`:429-455`) already state this exact two-opposite-fail-directions problem. Extend it; do not build a parallel record. CLAUDE.md §11.

🔴 THE HOLE, NAMED BY THE CODE ITSELF (`AccessibleRecordSetService.cs:443-455`, a task-043 code-review correction): "`Unreadable` is set only for faults that reach `ReadActiveOrgMembershipsAsync` as an EXCEPTION, which in practice means token/API-url acquisition. A junction QUERY failure (HTTP 500/403, timeout, any non-success status) is swallowed inside `ExternalParticipationService`'s private query, which returns an empty list — so it arrives here as `Unreadable: false` and the deny veto's ORGANIZATION axis silently stops matching for that subject. Contact-keyed deny rows still apply, so the wall narrows rather than vanishes. That hole PRE-DATES task 043 … fixing it properly has to happen ONE LAYER DOWN, in the junction query itself." That sentence is your specification. ISS-019's own suggested fix (a second `QueryActiveOrgIdsOutcomeAsync`) is REJECTED — it would restore the two-snapshot hazard.

⚠️ THE ASYMMETRY IS DELIBERATE — PRESERVE IT. `ExternalParticipationService.cs:1060-1069` says so in as many words: token/API-url acquisition is deliberately NOT guarded so it propagates to `ResolveDenyVetoAsync`'s catch-all (veto denies every candidate — correct for a veto), while a query-level fault fails toward an empty list (correct for the ADDITIVE caller — a fault must not GRANT). "Both are deliberate for their respective callers, not an inconsistency to 'fix' by unifying them." Your change closes only the query-level-fault-for-the-VETO hole. It must not make the additive term grant on a fault, and it must not make the veto's existing fail-closed path weaker.

🔴 A TRAP — TWO COLUMNS, OPPOSITE NULL SEMANTICS. `ExpiryPredicate` (`:99-100`) is `(sprk_expiresdate eq null or sprk_expiresdate ge {today:yyyy-MM-dd})`, and its remarks explain that in OData `field ge X` EXCLUDES nulls, so the `eq null` branch is load-bearing, and `ge` not `gt` because `sprk_expiresdate` is Date Only and access holds THROUGH the expiry date. Mirror the `ge`/null MECHANICS for `sprk_enddate` — a null end date means the membership has no end and STILL CONFERS. ⚠️ Task 107 (sibling) INVERTS the null branch for `sprk_expiresdate` so an undated GRANT confers nothing. Do NOT propagate that inversion to `sprk_enddate`: a grant with no expiry is an unbounded grant, a membership with no end date is an ordinary current membership. An executor "being consistent" across both columns would silently revoke every open-ended membership in the system.

THE THREE TERMS, AND WHICH ONE IS ALREADY GATED (my exposure claim was FALSE once — verify, do not inherit): the org-EXPANSION term IS standing-gated (`:1209-1221`), but the older org-GRANT term is NOT — `QueryOrganizationGrantRowsAsync` (`:1011-1051`) gates on active + unexpired GRANT ROWS only, with no `sprk_standinggrant` check. Live org grants existed (`notes/task-020-org-grant-spe-cleanup.md:18`). Real exposure is bounded only by dev being a test environment.

THREE ARTIFACTS CURRENTLY ASSERT THE RETIRED RULE — fix them in this change or they pin it (task 114's lesson):
1. `ExternalParticipationService.cs:1081-1082` — "a former member is a deactivated row and is excluded, so leaving a firm drops inherited access." FALSE under D-2 part 1: a date-ended but state-active row still confers.
2. `ExternalParticipationService.cs:1083-1085` — an undeleted caveat ("confirm against the created junction schema") that TASK-INDEX claims task 020 removed. The schema WAS live-verified on 2026-08-26; the record is at `Api/ExternalAccess/RevokeExternalAccessEndpoint.cs:659-665` (collection `sprk_contactorganizations`; `_sprk_contact_value` / `_sprk_organization_value`; `statecode` Active(0)/Inactive(1)).
3. `tests/Spaarke.ArchTests/ExternalAccessQueryIntegrityGuardTests.cs:54-57` — a GUARD TEST justifying its scope with "a correct query that rightly has no expiry predicate, because a membership row has no expiry. Expiry is a property of a GRANT." Now false, and load-bearing: it teaches the next reader the junction query needs no date predicate. Its cited line `:1068` is also stale — the junction query is at `:1092-1094`.

CONTRACT CHANGE, NOT A DEVIATION. The junction's original design contract (sibling project, `notes/task-073-org-grant-design.md:83, 90-92`) made `statecode` the SOLE active/former gate and never listed `sprk_enddate` as access-bearing. D-2 CHANGES that contract deliberately — which is why it was the owner's call and not an implementer's.

⚠️ ENVIRONMENT: the connected system is DEV and its records are TEST records (owner, 2026-09-17). That changes urgency, not validity.</background>
